KECAMATAN BATANG ALAI TIMUR KABUPATEN HULU SUNGAI TENGAH", "url": "https://ppjp.ulm.ac.id/journals/index.php/fag/article/view/618"}, {"title": "ikippgriptk.ac.id", "url": "https://jurnal.fipps.ikippgriptk.ac.id/index.php/GEOGRAFI/article/download/173/pdf"}, {"title": "nujuh jerami dan upaya pengakuan wilayah adat suku mapur di air abik", "url": "https://mongabay.co.id/2023/05/29/nujuh-jerami-dan-upaya-pengakuan-wilayah-adat-suku-mapur-di-air-abik/"}, {"title": "e-journal.upr.ac.id", "url": "https://e-journal.upr.ac.id/index.php/JBL/article/download/12039/7653/52757"}]}, "The search didn't return a list of tools used by Orang Mapur in shifting cultivation. It did turn up some related details.\n\n- **Planting method:** Mapur women plant rice in a communal way, and men make the planting holes. They scatter seeds into holes that the men make by driving a wooden stick into the ground. This suggests a digging stick, similar to the tugal used elsewhere, but the source doesn't name the tool.\n- **Current practice:** Mapur shifting cultivation in Air Abik is declining. Over the past 10 years, rice harvests from upland fields have dropped. Many have switched to planting oil palm or mining tin.\n- **Tools elsewhere:** A study of Dayak groups in Central Kalimantan describes shifting-cultivation implements such as karayan parei, utar, karuhei, and lisung, and notes that the tugal has both farming and spiritual significance. Most of these tools are being displaced or abandoned amid modernization, though they remain relevant to local knowledge preservation. This is about other communities, not the Mapur.\n\nFor the specific tool inventory, a search on Mapur (Suku Mapur) ethnographic studies or the Mongabay Bangka series might help."], "durationSeconds": 5.702225874999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01YXqTJFHustukoTYZyR7UAp", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
